From: plaz@netcom.com (Plaz)
Date: Sun, 27 Mar 1994 15:53:57 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-327] SOC: Nexi

Anna Yamada wrote a series of questions:

>        So, what are nexi,

A Nexus is any voluntary community of extropians. I usually leave out the
domiciles of extropian couples, but I'm not sure if that's fair. Anyway, we
didn't start calling ourselves Nexus-Lite until Nick moved in (making a
total of 3 extropians).

As I noted earlier in private e-mail, but will restate here, the word nexus
is going into general net usage as a house of folks who get together and
create an internet POP (Point of Presence) at thier place of residence.
This is becoming more common, due to the current economics of
high-bandwidth interenet access.

> and how is one set up,

Well, NexMistress Romana rented a five bedroom house. She's been subletting
four of the bedrooms. Eventually the extropians took the available rooms
and we got ourselves a nexus.

> and why did the last one break up,

In the spirit of self-transformation Extropians grow and change. I expect
any Nexus will be a temporary thing.

The last nexus broke up because Alcor moved to Arizona for business
reasons. Since three of four Nexans were employed by Alcor, changes
obviously hadto be made.

> and was it a bunch of geeky guys, and

Heavens no. I hope the various ex-riverside nexans will forgive my
characterizations, but they're a pretty sexy bunch.

Derek & Ralph are musicians, Derek's been transforming himself into a hunk
from his previous teddy bear state. Ralph is a tall thin guy, but too
worldly to called a geek. After seeing Tanya in my last trip down, I'd have
to qualify her as a "babe" (with a brain even). And Max, president of ExI,
is a body building philosopher.

Most extropians have their geeky side. I certainly do. But one of the great
things about being around extropians is the diversity of interests. It's
hard to remain a "geek" when there are so many neet things to explore.

As usual, and most unfortunately, the men do outnumber the women by a large
factor in the Nexi.

>        was it fun,

Can't quite answer that on the behalf of the ex-nexans, but it was fun to
visit. Fun seems an inadequite word for my experiences in Nextropia.

> and what were its dues

Dues? We pay rent. We are considering hooking into the net, which would
probably end up as a utility fee.

> and how come they don't occur more?

Nexi are a spontaneous order. They occur when a bunch of people enjoy each
other's company enough to live together.

Romana's bording house strategy has contributed, by allowing extropians we
don't know personally to stay and try out the "community".

>        I think extropians living in intentional community--say a farm
>        with all the hi-tech toys and knowledge and ideas percolating
>        would be a very interesting place to live, and probably would
>        be very fecund in all sorts of ways.
>
>        Any chance anyone on this list has a farm somewhere and
>        a slip connection and a satellite dish and a radio transmitter
>        who would care to set up one and front me the plane fare for
>        me and my kitty to be the first ones to show up and help open
>        house?
>
>        seriously.

I would recommend that any group get to know each other better before
starting. At least get a core group of two or three people.

PS - Anna, I am noting a somewhat isolationist and communitarian viewpoint
on your part. The Nexi have never been communes. In spite of my thoughts on
creating a tropical nexus someday, extropians are motivated by economics
and live were they can make money.

Perhaps someday location won't be as big a factor in money making ability,
but it is now. I can't afford to live on a farm.

Besides, I have no intention of being WACO'd.

