> And, by empirical evidence, to some dedicated observers.  How about some
> ideas about where to focus, what points should look like they're on top of
> each other, or something?

   Ok, Here's how I do it. First, remove any glasses/contacts. Second,
learn to be able to cross your eyes and switch your focus. For instance,
hold a finger in front of your eyes and learn to focus past the
finger on the wall, while still concentrating "viewing" the image of the
finger. This is the basic technique for viewing a SIRDS. What you must
do is look at the screen, but focus past it as if you were
trying to look at something on the wall behind the minitor. (or in
other words, become "wall eyed")

  Once you are comfortable manipulating your focus (becoming wall-eyed,
e.g. preventing your focus from changing), try this algorithm.

1) get as close to the screen as possible with your nose touching
2) go "wall-eyed", relax, don't try to focus on the words on the screen
3) now move your head slowly backwards *WITHOUT ALTERING YOUR FOCUS*
   once you get far from the screen, start moving forward again. Move
back and forward while wall-eyed until your eyes start seeing
a floating image in front of you. As soon as that happens, the SIRDS
should ``lock-in'' and you should be able to move your head around while
staring at the floating 3d image. It's really cool, but don't do it
for too long or you'll get a sort of motion sickness, or atleast
I do.

  The mandelbrot image I posted is not a really good starting point because
it's hard to see because it doesn't look good even when you see it. Try
ftping one of the black and white gifs from the FTP site listed on
that web document. (I started with the starship enterprise one)

