Date: Sun, 27 Mar 1994 20:47:54 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-331] Ozone Depletion

> Just remember, extropians, that if the whole ozone layer were removed,
> it's equivalent to moving less than a thousand miles closer towards
> the equator.

This statement reveals a blithe and careless lack of understanding of
what you just said.  You aren't talking about one person moving here,
you're talking about figuratively moving _every point on the earth_.

For instance, a global temperature drop of 5 degrees Celsius
is sufficient to cause another ice age.  Moving a thousand miles away
from the equator is sufficient to cause such a drop in relative
temperature.  Imagine the "economic disruption" if most of North 
America were ground under by glaciers.

Therefore, your example points toward (but does not prove) the
opposite conclusion, namely that loss of the ozone layer would be
disastrous in the true sense.

The other fallacy of the anti-ozone arguments is that they are a 
set of self-cancelling propositions.  They go like this:

1) The ozone layer isn't being destroyed,
2) And it isn't important anyway.

recalling the classic example of a horse thief's reply on being accused:

1) I don't steal horses,
2) And anyway you have a crummy horse.

The ozone 'hole' is proven to exist, or all the diagrams I've seen
based on satellite measurement are an elaborate hoax.  Ozone is proven
to block UV light.  Excessive UV light is proven to cause cataracts
and skin cancer, or the US medical establishment is colluding with the
hat and sunscreen industries to perpetrate another elaborate hoax.

	I believe, therefore, that it is incumbent on you to prove 1)
or 2) beyond a shadow of a doubt.  The only statements I've seen so
far are on the order of "no, really, it isn't dangerous, these people
are bad scientists".  That's not enough for me to gamble with my life.

